(5. A bit about email) Tj
T*
(Source: Maurycy's Blog | Published: Fri, 06 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000) Tj
T*
(Link: https://maurycyz.com/misc/email/) Tj
T*
(Before you torch and pitchfork me, I'm aware most of this is simplified and doesn't go into) Tj
T*
(edge cases, even though the internet is composed entirely of edge cases. Otherwise, I'd be) Tj
T*
(writing a whole book. First, some terminology; A typical email will past through many different) Tj
T*
(components on it's way to the recipient: 1. Sender's MUA [Via SMTP on port 465] 2. Sending MSA) Tj
T*
(3. Sending MTA [Via SMTP on port 25] 4. Receiving MTA 5. Receiving LDA and message store [Via) Tj
T*
(IMAP] 7. Receiver's MUA \(These aren't all necessarily different machines or even different) Tj
T*
(programs, but it is helpful to give a name for each step.\) The mail user agent \(MUA\) runs on) Tj
T*
(the user's computer and is what they use to send or read messages. Let's say they send) Tj
T*
(something: that message will be uploaded to to the submission agent \(MSA\) which checks the) Tj
T*
(user's password before handing off to the mail transfer agent \(MTA\). The transfer agent's job) Tj
T*
(is to talk to the other mail servers and get the message to the receiving side's MTA. Once) Tj
T*
(received, it's given to the local delivery agent \(LDA\) to be stored until the recipient checks) Tj
T*
(their email. The name "LDA" is non-standard, but I use it to avoid having four different...) Tj
T*
